{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Resultant force: F = ma (N = kg × m/s²). Rearrange to find a = F/m or m = F/a",
   "Momentum (HT): p = mv (kg m/s). Conservation: total momentum before = total momentum after",
   "Force from momentum change (HT): F = Δp ÷ Δt. Longer time = smaller force",
   "Wave speed: v = f × λ. Rearrange to f = v/λ or λ = v/f"
  ],
  "title": "Newton's Laws, Momentum and Waves",
  "content": "<p>This lesson covers three equation families linked by Newton's laws.</p><p><strong>F = ma:</strong> the resultant force equals mass × acceleration. If the resultant force is zero (Newton's first law), the object stays at rest or moves at constant speed.</p><p><strong>Momentum (higher tier):</strong> p = mv (kg m/s). Momentum is conserved in a closed system. F = Δp/Δt — a longer collision time reduces peak force for the same change in momentum.</p><p><strong>Wave speed:</strong> v = fλ. Units: m/s = Hz × m. All electromagnetic waves travel at 3 × 10⁸ m/s in a vacuum.</p><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Not converting wavelength to metres before substituting into v = fλ</li><li>Confusing Newton's first law (zero resultant → constant speed) with Newton's second law (non-zero resultant → acceleration)</li></ul>"
 },
